        "title": "Modern Machine-Readable Codes and Marking Systems",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Modern codes are optimized for **machine reading**, not human reading. The shift from human-decoded systems (Morse, semaphore) to machine-decoded systems (barcodes, QR) happened in the 1970s with laser scanners and CCD cameras. The defining requirement: encode enough redundancy that physical damage, printing imperfections, or partial obstruction don't cause decoding failures."
